# The old spelling, refused, with the new one named — and then applied by the
# two tools that carry a diagnostic's own bytes.
#
# `//lib/money` used to be how you wrote the library's surface. It names no
# file, so it is no longer a module path at all, and the toolchain says so with
# `import-path-without-a-file` — and then says which file: the note and the
# `fix` edit both carry "//lib/money/lib.buri", derived by resolving the old
# path the old way rather than by appending a suffix to a string.
#
# That matters here in a way a synthetic example cannot show. This repository
# also has `lib/money/cents.buri`, so "//lib/money/cents" would have to become
# "//lib/money/cents.buri" while "//lib/money" becomes "//lib/money/lib.buri".
# One rule cannot produce both from the text; the resolver produces both from
# the layout.
#
# The last three steps are the fix being *acted on*, because a suggestion
# nobody can act on is not a fix. `textDocument/codeAction` is what an editor
# offers — the same `edits` the diagnostic carries, replayed verbatim — and
# `buri lint --fix` is the terminal's half of it. Then the build goes green
# without a hand edit anywhere in the case.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.

doc: "an import that names no file is refused, the diagnostic names the file it meant, and both the editor and lint --fix write it"

run {
  args: ["build", "//cmd/app"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "build.txt"
}

run {
  args: ["build", "//cmd/app", "--error-format=json", "--force"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "build.json"
}

# What an editor offers on the line: one quick fix, carrying the exact bytes.
run {
  args: ["lsp"]
  exit: 0
  stdin: "session.jsonl"
  stream: OUT
  golden: "session.txt"
}

# And the terminal's half of the same edit, applied.
run {
  args: ["lint", "--fix", "//cmd/app"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "fix.txt"
}

run {
  args: ["build", "//cmd/app"]
  exit: 0
}
